856 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Phylum Echinodermata)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
857 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Echinoderms include common seashore animals such as sea stars \(also known as "starfish"\), sand dollars, and sea urchins, along with hundreds of more exotic forms. An echinoderm is any member of the phylum Echinodermata of marine animals. The adults are recognizable by their radial symmetry, and include starfish, sea urchins, sand dollars, sea cucumbers, and sea lilies or "stone lilies.")
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
858 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Phylum Chordata)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
859 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The Phylum Chordata includes the well-known vertebrates \(fishes, amphibians, reptiles, birds, mammals\). A chordate is an animal of the phylum Chordata. All chordates possess 5 synapomorphies, or primary characteristics, at some point during their larval or adulthood stages that distinguish them from all other animals.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
860 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Three main characteristics of chordates:)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
861 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (1. Chordata is the most advanced animal phylum.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
862 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (2. A notochord, a solid unjointed rod, is present in chordates at some stages of life.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
863 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (3. A dorsal hollow nerve cord develops in the embryo and usually persists throughout life in most chordates.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
864 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (4. Gill slits are present at some stages of life.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
865 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Characteristics of Urochordata)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
866 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Possesses a notochord, a hollow nerve cord, and a post-anal tail.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
867 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Body has more than two cell layers and includes tissues and organs.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
868 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Has a U-shaped gut.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
869 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Body has no coelomic body cavity.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
870 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Body wholly enclosed in a 'tunic' of secreted protein and cellulose-like material.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
871 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (What is chordate metamerism?)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
872 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (In chordates, metamerism evolved independently for locomotion. Metamerism allowed the tail muscles to be arranged segmentally for the undulatory movement of the body.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
873 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Animals belonging to the subphylum Cephalochordates)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
874 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Cephalochordates include animals that possess all 5 synapomorphies, such as the notochord, dorsal hollow nerve cord, endostyle, pharyngeal slits, and a post-anal tail. An example of Cephalochordates is the Bahamas lancelet, Asymmetron lucayanum.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
875 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Sub-phylum Vertebrata General Characteristics)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
876 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Diversity of vertebrates: The subphylum Vertebrata contains all animals that possess backbones, gills, and a central nervous system in at least one phase of development. Vertebrates include amphibians, reptiles, mammals, and birds, as well as jawless fishes, bony fishes, sharks, and rays.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
877 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 298 0 R
/K [855 0 R 23]
/Pg 23 0 R
>>
endobj
878 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 299 0 R
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
