/T (ZB)
/E (ZB)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
881 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (What does Gymnosperm mean?)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
882 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (A gymnosperm is any member of the class or subdivision Gymnospermae, a group of woody vascular seed plants \(such as conifers or cycads\) that produce naked seeds not enclosed in an ovary. Some gymnosperms have motile spermatozoids.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
883 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Angiosperms Definition)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
884 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (An angiosperm is a plant that produces flowers. Angiosperms, also known as flowering plants, are a vital group of plants having seeds.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
885 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Definition of monocot)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
886 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (A monocot is a chiefly herbaceous angiospermous plant \(such as a grass, lily, or palm\) with a single cotyledon in the embryo, parallel-veined leaves, and floral organs arranged in multiples of three. Monocots account for a quarter of all flowering plants.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
887 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Dicots)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
888 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (A dicot, or dicotyledon, is a member of the flowering plants \(angiosperms\) that has a pair of leaves, or cotyledons, in the embryo of the seed.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
889 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Sexual Reproduction in Plants)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
890 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
891 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Development of the Ovule and Female Gamete)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
892 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The female gametophyte develops within the ovule and generally consists of three antipodal cells, one central cell, two synergid cells, and one egg cell. The female gametophyte is also called the embryo sac or megagametophyte.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
893 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Evolution of Gymnosperms)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
894 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Gymnosperms were the first seed plants to evolve. The earliest seed-like bodies are found in rocks of the Upper Devonian Series \(about 382.7 million to 358.9 million years ago\).)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
895 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Life Cycle of Gymnosperms)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
896 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (The life cycle of a gymnosperm involves alternation of generations, with a dominant sporophyte in which reduced male and female gametophytes reside. All gymnosperms are heterosporous, with male and female spores developing in different strobili.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
897 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Cycads)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
898 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Cycads \(order Cycadales\) are an ancient group of palmlike gymnosperms with about 300 extant species across three families. They are distinguished by crowns of large pinnately compound leaves and by large cones.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
